  "headline": "NO-GO: the pure-critical group law is refuted. geom_v2_a3_pure0_homog, which has pure_crit=0 and homogeneous yield ratios, returned a positive frontier advantage of +0.00494 [+0.00418, +0.00570] where both pre-registered hypotheses predicted null. The refutation survives correction for interpolation bias (+0.00398 after subtracting a leave-one-out bias of +0.00096). Within the exactly-matched A3 triple the advantage is graded rather than binary: pure_ge1 +0.01209, pure0_hetero +0.00594, pure0_homog +0.00398 after bias correction, so pure-criticality and ratio heterogeneity each contribute but neither is necessary.",
  "mechanism": "A post-hoc leave-one-out test showed the primary metric carries a systematic positive bias: interpolating a uniform grid point's own AUC from the remaining points, where the true value is exactly zero by construction, returns positive in 99.2 percent of cases with mean +0.00136. This is the concavity artifact of interpolating a concave AUC(log KL) curve linearly. It explains why partial_feature_aliasing flipped from the null measured on the geometry-v1 seeds to a marginal positive here (+0.00067, CI lower bound +0.00002): after bias correction it is -0.00147, consistent with the earlier null. The bias does not explain geom_v2_a3_pure0_homog.",
  "interpretation_limits": [
    "Every result rests on the softmax-linear one-hot tie-group parameterization of assumption (M1). The collapse identity, the convexity argument, and the exact projection all depend on it. Nothing here transfers to non-linear function approximation.",
    "Lemma 2 supplies a NECESSARY structural condition, not a sufficient one. In particular the observed null on partial_feature_aliasing is not explained by the theory: that scenario contains a purely-distractor tie-group whose down-weighting should reduce distractor KL at zero critical cost. The mean-normalization cancellation conjecture is not assumed and not tested by this protocol.",
    "The five inherited scenarios are seen data. Only the three A3 arms are new under this protocol.",
    "lambda*=3.0 and mu*=0.0 remain frozen from geometry-v1 and are not re-selected here. This protocol says nothing about their optimality.",
    "The heterogeneous discriminator arm cannot match the per-group critical-count multiset of the other two arms, because heterogeneous finite yield ratios at a fixed group-size multiset require unequal critical counts. Its evidence is correspondingly weaker than the exactly-matched homogeneous/pure-critical pair.",
    "No runtime interval may be quoted; wall-clock ratios do not reproduce across machines. See docs/ERRATA.md E7."
  ],
